cblink's libFuzzer harnesses
============================

The parsers that see what a peer or a person sends, each fuzzed with
libFuzzer under AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer (clang's
-fsanitize=fuzzer,address,undefined; FreeBSD's base clang has them).  They
are not part of the normal build.  From the top of the tree:

	make fuzz

builds every harness in obj/fuzz (make -C fuzz builds them in fuzz/
itself).  The library's sources are compiled into each harness; the YANG
harnesses decode by the committed tables of cblink-gen's test module
shapes (yshapes.h, yshapes_schema.c: "make -C fuzz regen" remakes them
with the cblink-gen built, and cblink_gen_test:ymod_regen fails when they
differ).

Harnesses and their seeds (corpus/<name>):

  fuzz_frame	a frame as it arrives: decoded, its tree walked, its
		top-level items found again, read as every control-family
		message, a notification, a GET; its error.  Seeds: frames
		of the control family and of the tests' families.
  fuzz_conn	a byte stream on an accepted connection (unix socketpair,
		integration mode): the receive ring, frames cut out of it,
		each dispatched to the control family or to a family of an
		echo (cmd 3), a dump (4) and an echoing stream (5), or taken
		as a reply or a stream frame; EOF at the end.  Seeds: the
		frame seeds, alone and after a hello, a whole session, and a
		stream with data.  The family is the first registered: id 2.
  fuzz_proxy	a PROXY protocol header (v1 and v2) read from a socket.
  fuzz_ycodec	YANG-CBOR decoded into every struct of shapes, strictly
		and not; what decodes validated and round-tripped, also
		through the instance tree.  Seeds: every struct, filled.
  fuzz_yjson	JSON: the reader (cbl_json.c); RFC 7951 JSON of shapes
		into YANG-CBOR by the translator (cbl_yxlate.c), as an
		object of top-level nodes and, after a first byte that picks
		the node, as one node's value; back to JSON and into the
		same CBOR; a .sid file (RFC 9595 JSON).  Seeds: the ycodec
		seeds as JSON, a shapes container with every leaf, the
		shapes .sid file.
  fuzz_iid	paths: an instance-identifier in YANG-CBOR (cbl_yiid_read)
		and as XPath (cbl_yxlate_iid), a schema-node path
		(cbl_ysids_data), a NACM rule path (cbl_dnacm_path_new,
		matched against a tree and the schema), and, after a first
		byte that picks a leaf, a value from its canonical string
		(cbl_yvalue_parse).  Seeds: xpathNN and their CBOR cborNN,
		nacmN, valueN, and the two inputs it found (a list without
		its keys, a key not UTF-8).
  fuzz_xpath	an XPath expression: parsed, printed, parsed again to the
		same print, evaluated over a tree of shapes.
  fuzz_pattern	an XSD pattern, translated to an ERE and compiled.

Running one
-----------

New inputs go to the first corpus directory given: give a scratch one
first, so that the committed seeds stay as they are, and keep crash
files out of the tree with -artifact_prefix:

	mkdir -p /tmp/fz/frame
	nice -n 10 obj/fuzz/fuzz_frame -max_total_time=600 \
	    -artifact_prefix=/tmp/fz/frame- /tmp/fz/frame fuzz/corpus/frame

Each harness takes the same options (fuzz_<name> -help=1 lists them).
Useful ones:

  -max_total_time=N	stop after N seconds
  -jobs=N -workers=N	N processes (-fork=N was seen to hang on FreeBSD)
  -max_len=N		inputs up to N bytes (fuzz_conn takes up to 64 KB)
  -print_final_stats=1	runs, executions per second, peak RSS
  -runs=0 -print_coverage=1 dir	what the seeds in dir reach

For the long runs of harnesses with many small inputs (fuzz_xpath,
fuzz_pattern, fuzz_iid), ASAN_OPTIONS=quarantine_size_mb=16 keeps
AddressSanitizer's quarantine of freed memory from reaching libFuzzer's
RSS limit.  LeakSanitizer does not work on FreeBSD: a leak shows only as
RSS that keeps growing over a run (-rss_limit_mb, 2048 by default).

.github/ci/sanitize.sh fuzz runs every harness for FUZZ_TIME seconds
(default 60), as CI does.

A crash
-------

libFuzzer writes the input to <artifact_prefix>crash-<sha1> (or leak-,
timeout-, oom-) and prints the sanitizer's report.  Rerun it alone:

	obj/fuzz/fuzz_frame /tmp/fz/frame-crash-<sha1>

then shrink it with -minimize_crash=1 -runs=100000.  Fix it with a
regression test in the suite that covers that code (lib/libcblink/tests
for the wire, usr.bin/cblink-gen/tests for the YANG codec, the
translator and the paths), and add the input to corpus/<name> when it
reaches code no seed does.
